CLASystems@gmail.com\par
\par
Introduction.\par
\par
All changes to P?S/8 Version 8Z (since the initial release on several devices) are logged in this file.\par
\par
23-Feb-1988\tab Initial system creation.\par
\par
This is the date of the initial release of P?S/8 Version 8Z.\par
\par
This does not include updates to various components created somewhat later.  The relevant files are currently under review; however, the only known issues relate to the simultaneous use of the Logical Console Overlay configured for the VT8E and FOCAL customized with the VT8E-oriented VT8PCH binary patch file.  This issue has been tabled pending a more general release of the system.\par
\par
13-Nov-2014\tab Version 8Z for MDC8 diskette (FLP) image recovered.\par
\par
This image was recovered from a working diskette for P?S/8 Version 8Z configured for the MDC8 5.25" diskette drives (FLP).\par
\par
Most importantly, the .DUMP files that are used to generate system components beyond the initial starter system were recovered from this image after successfully transferring it to the SIMH environment.\par
\par
Note:  A series of utilities was required to create the SIMH version of this image.  The files were accessed and converted using existing bootable images of P?S/8 Version 8T.\par
\par
All known variant system-generation .DUMP files were recovered including the common file used to extend the starter system (created from binary files of the relevant source files) to a (nearly) complete system.\par
\par
Note: Several additional system components are generally added to full releases to include support for specific hardware such as media formatting or copying programs.\par
\par
The .DUMP file used to complete the FLP system is essentially the same as the similar file used for TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 systems with the exception of the device length (the capacity of the FLP system diskettes exceeds that of standard or premium DECtapes).\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\cf0\par
26-Nov-2014\tab P?S/8 Version 8Z "A" source files recovered.\par
\par
All known source files for P?S/8 that are part of the "A" collection (meant to be placed on an \i RKAx\i0 : drive or equivalent) were recovered from OS/8 format MDC8 5.25" diskettes.  Image files were transferred to SIMH and all files were checked for consistent contents and file validity.  These files have also been transferred to MS-DOS/Windows equivalent directories with full file names (OS/8 file naming limitations have been removed).\cf1\par
\par
\cf0 13-Dec-2014\tab P?S/8 Version 8Z "B" source files recovered.\par
\par
All known source files for P?S/8 that are part of the "B" collection (meant to be placed on an \i RKBx\i0 : drive or equivalent) were recovered from OS/8 format MDC8 5.25" diskettes.  Image files were transferred to SIMH and all files were checked for consistent contents and file validity.  These files have also been transferred to MS-DOS/Windows equivalent directories with full file names (OS/8 file naming limitations have been removed).\par
\par
VT8E and MDC8 issues.\par
\par
At some point later, the VT8E-related source files mentioned above were recovered along with many other files unrelated to P?S/8 (apparently as part of a general backup of an OS/8 development drive based on the MDC8).  These files are not part of the general release of P?S/8 per se; they are needed by users who configure the Logical Console Overlay and/or P?S/8 FOCAL for use with the VT8E DMA terminal device; binary files produced from these files are passed to the respective program for customization purposes.  Future releases of\line P?S/8 may include these updated source files; at this time, it is not known if this work was finalized (or even tested).\par
\par
As of this writing, nearly all P?S/8 source files have been recovered with notable exceptions related to the MDC8 Winchester (hard drive) system, the two variant LINC-8 system configurations supported, and all files related to the TD8E both with and without the MR8E-C ROM.\par
\par
The MDC8 Winchester source files can be derived from other source files already recovered, notably the P?S/8 MDC8 FLP system and non-system device handlers, and the OS/8 MDC8 system and non-system device handlers for the FLP and Winchester systems.\par
\par
Note: All MDC8 device handlers are quite elegant due to the exceptional nature of the hardware (which was designed to avoid software issues).  As such, it is not that much effort to restore any missing (or new) handlers as necessary.  Other than the usual relationships between P?S/8 and OS/8 regarding logical blocks of 128 words/block versus records of 256 words/block, an advanced feature was added to allow a master offset of an entire memory field storage before starting all device handlers (in either system).\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\cf0\par
\par
The purpose of this logical offset is to implement the essential element of MENU-8, a \ul\i Master Menu\ulnone\i0  system vaguely reminiscent of the actual utility system implemented on the DECmate II and DECmate III+ systems using hard disks; however, DECmate systems include hardware support of named volumes to isolate the menu system from the subordinate operating systems.  On the MDC8, a logical sector offset allows sufficient code space to implement the most primitive functions of a menu-based system, including configuring where on the device the rest of the menu system is located based on absolute logical blocks states as a 24-bit logical block value.\line\line Because the MDC8 logical capacity includes a reserved area at the highest physical blocks, the rest of MENU-8 is intended to be located just before the blocks reserved for the bad block management area on the disk.  In general, this represents a small percentage of the overall device capacity; as such, MENU-8 reserving slightly more disk space is not a significant issue.\line\line While MENU-8 was never completed, the source code was also recovered with the VT8E-related files mentioned above.  The work can be continued should appropriate hardware-related circumstances arise.  (For new work on PDP-8 storage devices, the MDC8 is the recommended device to emulate assuming single-cycle DMA transfers.)\line\line As currently implemented, the MDC8 will boot into the first logical sector, which could contain the boot block of either P?S/8 or OS/8 assuming the respective source code files are assembled with the offset disabled.  If the offset is enabled in these handlers, it is assumed that the MENU-8 reboot code is present, configured to point to the desired operating system located further up the device.  Because of the logical addressing, well-rounded values are generally used as starting logical block addresses (if the offset is disabled) and one track higher-still (if the offset is enabled).  All configurations on any particular hard drive must be consistent (either way).\line\line The only handler source code currently missing is the P?S/8 system device handler for the MDC8 hard drive.  Fortunately, it shares much with the OS/8 system device handler (which was recovered along with several of the other source files mentioned above).  Thus, it is fairly easy to recover the original handler by using the P?S/8 system device handler from the FLP: system coupled with the OS/8 system device handler.  All of the relevant geometrical definitions are identically contained in the two handlers, thus the recovery can be easily completed with minimum effort.  Note: The equivalent non-system handler was never written; however this can also be derived from the FLP: non-system handler.  All of these handlers represent themes and variations on each other.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\cf0\par
\par
LINC-8 and TD8E issues.\par
\par
Bootable LINC-8 systems are available on surviving LINCtapes.  The missing source files can be recovered by a medium effort of binary disassembly; additionally, certain related utilities are available in source form (which can help with certain code fragments).\par
\par
All P?S/8 TD8E source files are presently unavailable.  Assuming the files are never recovered, programming for this device will require new work, in part based on the author's recollections.\par
\par
Note: A partial recovery of TD8E support has been initiated using a recovered P?S/8 TD8E with MR8E-C ROM DECtape image.  It is not clear if this is the final version of this configuration.  The author acknowledges this particular system variant was produced under expedient circumstances with known defects.  For the present, it is presumed this will be the best available recovered source code fragment; disassembly has produced a working copy that documents the known defective behavior.\par
\par
Additionally, a set of specific workarounds for this aspect of this system have been proposed but not finalized pending overall source code cleanup; it is not certain the corrected source code will be compatible with P?S/8 Version 8Z, as most new work in ongoing projects is targeted to a next-generation Version 9A system which has slightly different internal features.  Considering the changes that will be required to produce Version 9A compatibility, it is deemed of little value to produce an interim release for Version 8Z.  Additionally, some of the recovery work will affect a TD8E with extended memory variant system, as certain components are either partially or fully compatible across both configurations.\par
\par
It is known that the missing files were never part of the "A" and "B" file collections due to lack of sufficient storage space at the various development sites used at the time.  Backup copies of these files may show up as part of an ongoing recovery process.\par
\par
All present efforts will be focused on releasing other P?S/8 configurations that are achievable using the various source files, most notably the TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 DECtape system, the TC12 LINCtape system, the RX01 system and the RK8E/RK05 system.  Several other sets of handler source code exists for somewhat obscure third-party devices such as the DSD-240-8/Western Dynex fixed/removable disk cartridge system.\par
\par
The most appropriate system to implement first is a modern release of the TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 system; surviving hardware is available in several locations and there is excellent support for this device within the SIMH environment.  Many internal aspects of P?S/8 development must be dealt with before any semblance of a workable system can be created.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
\cf0\par
15-May-2015\tab Hacking up a bootable system for the TC01/TC08 DECtape.\par
\par
Using P?S/8 Version 8U for TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 (available on the SIMH simulator), the system device handler for the TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 was kludged onto to a copy of the image of the Version 8Z FLP system.  Predictably, many functions do not work properly; with some manual patching of the directory, a viable temporary system was created.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
Most importantly, access to certain newer system programs is now possible to aid in a proper reconstruction.  The image of the Version 8Z FLP system can be accessed as a mounted logical unit (such as unit six).\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 Version 8T is quite incompatible with P?S/8 Version 8Z. The only functional level of compatibility is the keyboard monitor commands (while generally different) are equivalent to those in the newer system allowing access to the TFS directory on the newer system.\par
\par
15-Jun-2015\tab Bootable startup system for the TC01/TC08 DECtape.\par
\par
Various OS/8 BATCH files are available within the "A" and "B" file collections to assemble the binary files needed to create a P?S/8 release.  By studying the BATCH files, it was determined what source files are needed to create what is referred to as the P?S/8 "starter" system.\par
\par
The starter system is a bootable operating system with limited capability configured for the target device.  Creation of the starter system depends on the ability to run the target system device handler for the purpose of creating a clean image of itself (and other system components) from a working copy moved to the intended operating memory locations (07600-07777).  An internal system generation routine is used, essentially a series of calls to write out system component sections loaded into most memory locations of a 12K machine; the system generation routine is aware of the loading address of all components involved in the system generation (which has nothing to do with their ultimate execution address when called into memory for their normal operating function).\par
\par
Note: Certain system device configurations require an extended memory device handler extension for normal operation.  In some cases, the complication of creating a functioning system device handler for the intended purpose may be cumbersome.  Future releases will avoid this problem by supplying an associated non-system device handler for the identical configuration.  In most instances, the non-system device handler will occupy less memory than the routines needed to force temporary viability of the system device handler.  Additionally, in certain specific cases, the source code can be simplified to allow internal documentation that is more appropriate to the resultant coding.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
\cf0\par
The starter system is obtained by loading the following binary files in the proper order as follows:\par
