25-Jun-2015\tab Problems completing the common system generation.\par
\par
While not all components of the OS/8 BATCH command files have been tested to determine if a complete collection of required binary files can be obtained, a problem has already been identified:\par
\par
All components of every released configuration of P?S/8 beyond the starter system must be created on an OS/8 non-system device such as \i DTA1\i0 :.  Even if a specific program is not required for any particular system, error-free assembly of all components must be enforced.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
\cf0\par
The reason for this is that the OS/8 non-system device will create core-image copies of all of the P?S/8 components involved; the specific OS/8 records where these files are stored is crucial.  The .DUMP files are extremely pragmatic; the most minor change will corrupt the process.\par
\par
With two notable exceptions, all of the P?S/8 source files are compatible with PAL8 Version B0 which is the standard version released with \i OS/278\i0  Version 2.0.  To within a minor operational bug that never affects most users, this version will function correctly under earlier systems such as \i OS/8\i0  Version 3D, considered the standard release for all PDP-8 systems.  (The bug has to do with the \i TTY\i0 : handler and interaction with the \highlight1 Control-Z\highlight0  character; the problem is completely avoidable by observing proper timing or simply not using the \i TTY\i0 : handler.)\line\line Note: The original release of PAL8 associated with OS/8 Version 3D is not recommended for use; however, available patches can produce a version that is adequate for the purpose of P?S/8 system generation.  The OS/278 version of PAL8 is widely available since it is included in the Kermit-12 collection written by the author of this document.  Moreover, Kermit-12 is meant to be assembled on DECmates; as such, the requirements for Kermit-12 include the need for one universal release version reasonably compatible with all possible systems Kermit-12 runs on.  No release of PAL8 from OS/8 Version 3D runs on DECmates.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
Two programs, \i MARK12\i0  and \i TC12F\i0 , are adaptations of standard components of the \ul\i LAP6-DIAL/DIAL-MS\ulnone\i0  system for the PDP-12 only.  The source code was taken from a specific release of \i DIAL-MS\i0  and is likely not the final release code (nor is this even important).  Apparently there are no operational differences between this code and the standard versions found on any newer \i DIAL\i0  systems per se; however, these programs have been modified to be more suitable for use with 128 words/block LINCtapes (which are used by P?S/8 and OS/8 on the PDP-12 and the LINC-8) with the ability to format longer LINCtapes assuming premium media is available.  As such, these programs must be supported as written, if possible.\par
\par
Note: Custom modifications exist in the P?S/8 version of the programs to overcome \i DIAL\i0 -specific issues which are in conflict with PDP-8 memory loading conventions.  When these programs are loaded into\par
memory, the P?S/8 kernel is destroyed.  As such, manual bootstrap procedures are required after using either program.\par
\par
When P?S/8 system generation was being performed in 1988, these programs were assembled with a specific user-written assembler known as \i PAL12\i0 .\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
\par
\cf0\i PAL12\i0  is based on a very early release of \i PAL8\i0  (that is quite deficient).  Despite major shortcomings, the LINC mode assembly facility is an acceptable implementation of the \i DIAL\i0  embedded assembler LINC mode subsection.  Very little PDP-8 code is used in these programs; as such, This particular \i PAL12\i0  release is an\par
acceptable assembler for this limited purpose.\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 PAL Version 8S (as released as a component of P?S/8 Version 8Z) is a proper superset of both \i PAL8\i0  and \i PAL12\i0 ; however, it is not an OS/8 program.\par
\par
Unfortunately, attempts to locate this specific release of \i PAL12\i0  has not been successful to date:\par
\par
Earlier releases of \i PAL12\i0  were located that cannot assemble the programs due to lacking certain assembler features added later.  Apparent file dates confirm these are precursors to the stable release required.\line\line Unfortunately, the \i PAL12\i0  project was turned over to other programmers who have a very poor understanding of exactly how a proper LINC mode assembler should operate.  While the features missing from the earlier releases are present (and many mostly irrelevant features of PAL8 are more like newer releases), the ability to assemble most LINC mode instructions is hopelessly incompatible other than some simplistic subsets; the newer release is essentially worthless for practical assembly of many LINC programs such as TC12F and MARK12.\par
\par
A binary copy of OS/8 for the modified 8K LINC-8 has been located.  This includes LINC-8 handlers loaded into a copy of OS/8 \i BUILD\i0 .  This LINCtape is likely the only surviving OS/8 LINCtape for the LINC-8 in the world due to its dependency on hardware modifications that were only applied to a single 8K LINC-8.  (P?S/8 runs on at least one other 4K LINC-8 with the same modifications.)  A paper directory indicates the proper \i PAL12\i0  release may be found on this tape.  Once a reasonable recovery process can be completed, the issue of producing the common system components (and system-specific add-ons) will be revisited.\line\line Note: Fortunately, example LINC-8 LINCtape programs exist that take advantage of the same hardware modifications as used in these handlers (and the P?S/8 analogous handlers); as such, it is not really a major programming problem to recreate these handlers (and actually improve the performance further).\line\line It is the author's recollection that the OS/8 handler used on this unique OS/8 bootable LINC-8 LINCtape does not depend on all of the available instructions provided by the final version of the modifications.  As such, it is highly likely that even if the source code is reconstructed, it can be improved to add useful features.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
\cf0\par
Note: Some programming effort (using the SIMH PDP-8 simulator) has been performed modifying the \i MARK12\i0  program in an attempt to make the source code compatible with the newer (albeit defective) release of \i PAL12\i0 .  In theory, after a lot of recoding effort, the source code for the two affected programs could produce proper binary files for use in\par
the P?S/8 system generation process.  However, the two affected system programs are not present on the recovered P?S/8 Version 8Z (FLP) system; existing binary copies are needed to confirm the source code changes produce the intended binary output.  A P?S/8 Version 8R system for TC12 has also been recovered.  If a copy of the core image of the programs on this LINCtape can be transferred to the SIMH environment, the source code modification process can continue with confidence.\par
\par
27-Jun-2015\tab Hacking up a complete P?S/8 Version 8Z system for TC08.\par
\par
It has been determined that the P?S/8 Version 8Z (FLP) system is essentially the same as the TC08 system with regard to the process of creating the common system generation and system-specific extensions.  The only important difference is ensuring the TFS directory length is appropriate to the TC08 system.\par
\par
Note: This was determined by inspection of the FLP-related system-specific extensions .DUMP file and the TC01/TC08-related system-specific extensions .DUMP file.\par
\par
P?S/8 \i BLKCPY\i0  was run by booting to the starter system with the hacked-up TC08 (but mostly FLP) system on logical device unit 0 while also mounting an exact copy imaged onto a DECtape file image mounted on logical unit six.  This allows the major sections that would be created by the .DUMP files to be copied en masse from the blocks on the image on device unit 6 to the corresponding blocks on the booted device unit 0.\par
\par
Note: While P?S/8 is fully compatible with all standard bootstraps on all standard devices, the TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 Version 8Y and newer systems are also compatible with \i TCBOOT\i0 , an extended DECtape startup program that allows P?S/8 to boot to any DECtape drive unit in the range of 0 through 7.  TCBOOT can also boot most PDP-8 operating systems for the same configuration as long as drive unit 0 is selected.  While fully functional, usage outside of booting P?S/8 for TC01/TC08 on non-zero logical units is not recommended since shorter bootstraps exist for drive 0 only.\par
\par
A copy of \i TCBOOT.PAL\i0  is available from Charles Lasner; this version requires manual drive unit selection via the front panel switches; an automatic version can be derived that will detect the first drive available in a manner analogous to the extended \i RXBOOT\i0  program for RX01/RX02/RX03/DSD-210/RX278.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
\cf0\par
Certain structures were also copied using P?S/8 \i DUMP\i0  in manual command mode, such as copying the system programs directory; this allows the newly-added systems program to run directly on the newly-created TC08 system.\par
\par
After confirming (by direct inspection with P?S/8 DUMP) that all directory structures are consistent with the TC01/TC08 system, the first P?S/8 Version 8Z system for TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 in over twenty-five years has been produced!\par
\par
10-Jul-2015\tab P?S/8 PAL bug fix.\par
\par
Problem: P?S/8 PAL creates defective binary files only during an obscure scenario involving the simultaneous creation of binary files and listing output at the same time.  If literals are in use and there are no current page literals on the last page of code and page zero literals exist to be dumped because there is a FIELD directive in the source code, the field change associated with the new field setting will be created incorrectly.  The problem had apparently been present for many releases prior to P?S/8 PAL Version 8S, but was never observed before due to the requirement of so many specific conditions simultaneously.\par
\cf1\par
\cf0 The problem was traced to an improper use of a subroutine header used as a counter.  It is actually unavailable in the specific circumstances described above.  (The technique is valid per se, just not in these specific circumstances.)\par
\par
Resolution: As a temporary fix, both references to the temporary (\i XP45CH\i0 ) will be patched to instead use an available page zero location;  Future releases will reorganize the code to prevent the problem.\par
\par
