01-Aug-2015\tab Date year base group update to 2008.\par
\par
In P?S/8 Version 8Z, the date is partially determined by the present value of the implicit base year group.  The base year group is calculated as the count of 8 year increments past 1960; when P?S/8 Version 8Z was originally released, the value used was 3 corresponding to a valid year range of 1984 through 1994.\par
\par
Note: While the internal implementation of the date allows a theoretical range of somewhat over 11 years, the P?S/8 \i DATE\i0  utility only allows valid dates in the present 11 year range to avoid unexpected date expiration during the next (partial) year following.\par
\par
The base year values are implemented as internal locations within all programs that support the present date; the relative date within the 11 year range is stored in the system kernel as the contents of location 07610.  The system kernel value is updated by the \i DATE\i0  command; the other programs generally support printing today's date by reading the current date value in memory and calculating the overall date using the locally-stored base year group value within the image of the program.  As such, all such programs require a binary patch to reflect the desired base year group value.\par
\par
The replacement value to be applied is 6, which corresponds to a date range of 2008 through 2018.  If desired, the base year group value can be updated in the future to 7 to correspond to a date range of 2016-2026.\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 Version 9A will use an entirely different method of implementing the system date.  The entire date will be available in the system kernel; the maintenance procedure described here will become moot and completely obsolete.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
