\cf0 17-May-2017\tab Certain P?S/8 System Device Handlers Located.\par
\par
A significant P?S/8 system device handler file recovery in the form of a single very long source file has been found.\line\line Prior to P?S/8 Version 8V, a misguided attempt to keep every handler and related component in one file along with all the other source code the components interacted with (primarily the keyboard monitor) was the chosen method to create (most of) the P?S/8 starter system for an6 particular supported device.\line\line Eventually, it was realized this is highly impractical and the practice was abandoned.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\cf0\line\line The usual "portable" approach to developing P?S/8 was carried out in the form of an "A" and a "B" collection.  This refers to the likelihood that sites where work might be accomplished (including the possibilities of an available lineprinter) were typically organized as either RK05 packs or alternate hardware with comparable disk geometry.  (Note: OS/8 actually allows logical devices nearly 50% larger, but none of these larger devices were relevant at the time.)\line\line In turn, the "A" and "B" files were stored on three designated DECtapes each; OS/8 BATCH control files were developed to manage this file collection, etc.\line\line Unfortunately, eventually the full complement of source code files of P?S/8 could not fit into this structure, and this ultimately (and indirectly) caused certain problems, including the need to recover several specific handlers.\par
\par
The MDC8 P?S/8 hard drive handler ("Winchester") was never included in this collection, in part because the author's personal system had grown to be more than adequate, and primarily based on the MDC8 itself.  As such, the OS/8 logical volumes were the largest allowed in OS/8 (and thus nearly 50% larger than the RK05 logical size) where this was no longer a problem (but was prohibitive in terms of backup DECtapes).\line\line To date, while this hard drive handler has not been recovered (but the process to do so using all of the other recovered handlers, including all of the OS/8 equivalents), is an easily accomplished task given the high degree of documentation of the other handlers and the elegant simplicity of the MDC8 itself.  Thus, MDC8 issues post-date any of these considerations.\line\line The very large file \i PQSMON.OL\i0  was located on a premium-length DECtape as the only file present (with few spare blocks past the file).\line\line Analyzing this file yields certain results as follows:\line\line a) All of the then-present handlers (some not even complete!) are\par
   present; the file appears to correspond to P?S/8 Version 8U.\line\line b) A string of conditional assembly variables are used to choose which\par
   particular system should be assembled.  This roughly corresponds to\par
   the current method of creating P?S/8 for the same components.\par
\par
   Note: The current method has been expanded to also include adding\par
   in the P?S/8 DUMP and P?S/8 ODT components to create a more viable\par
   starter system capable of being patched when booted to; this would\par
   be part of a potential fixup when upgrading a starter system to a\line    more complete system by adding common system programs.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\cf0\line\line c) Conditionals exist to create a variety of "lost" systems including\par
   the unmodified LINC-8 requiring 8K, the modified LINC-8 requiring\par
   4K (or more), the TD8E with 8K or more and the TD8E with 4K and the\par
   MR8E-C ROM (located at the end of memory in field 7 7400-7777).\par
\par
   Additionally, several other systems are present (such as the TC08)\par
   which are already available as modular system devices; when\par
   creating a starter system, the appropriate device handler complex\par
   is loaded along with the other binary files to create a starter\par
   system for that device, etc.\par
\line    While the recovery of these source file sections is extremely\par
   important, further work is needed to make use of them:\par
\par
a) There are some internal differences between Version 8U and Version\par
   8Z that must be resolved.  This is considered a moderate amount of \par
   work (but hardly insurmountable).  Much of this has to do with the\par
   newer modular approach and the specific environment described by\par
   the present starter system scenario.\line\line    Note: The entire method of creating P?S/8 is subject to change\par
   involving using some never-before-used methods that are now under\par
   consideration.\par
\par
b) All modular P?S/8 system device handler source files include\par
   interception routines associated with the Logical Console Overlay.\par
   At the time of this earlier work (nearly six years before Version\par
   8Z), this was not (yet) an established method.  As such, each\par
   handler must have new work applied to be fully compatible.\line\line c) A nearly-functioning P?S/8 system for the TD8E and MR8E-C has been\par
   recovered (and booted both on the actual hardware and on the SIMH\par
   simulator).  This has led to an understanding there is an\par
   unresolved major problem that some work has been done on to achieve\par
   a successful replacement device handler.  Assembling the identical\par
   configuration as used in the \i PQSMON.OL\i0  file produces the same exact\par
   system device handler (and identical bugs).\line\line    The author recollects a special interaction between the TD8E and\par
   the vT8E-specific variant of the Logical Console Overlay.  This\par
   involves limiting the DMA overhead of the VT8E to an 8 character\par
   display of the word \i WAITING\i0  using blink attributes on every\par
   character, etc.  To avoid rederiving old work, the recovery\par
   process has to continue to investigate this particular DECtape\par
   image.\line\line This P?S/8 system is from a time where two major differences apply:\line\line a) There is no way to determine the exact release revision.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\cf0\line\line b) The Logical Console Overlay was not created originally in the\par
   present form.  This system has the forerunner \i VT\i0  program that is\par
   VT8E-specific and not capable of configuration.\line\line    As such, disassembly of the VT program will be required; hopefully,\par
   this feature can be located within the VT code, and then recreated\par
   as an add-on to the current Logical Console Overlay.\line\line    Note: This issue is only related to the TD8E hardware due to the\par
   critical timing nature of the (primitive) DECtape controller).\par
\par
   From the author's recollection, it is not likely the VT program\par
   interaction was ever applied to the TD8E (with extended memory)\par
   variation.  There will be some differences between the two\par
   variant systems that may require slightly different implementation.\par
\par
   When using the extended-memory variant implementation, all device\par
   handler code can be intercepted as required; the ROM version\par
