   the relevant system blocks were copied to the actual RX01 media.\par
   Certain blocks were copied from the TC01/TC08 system which includes\par
   all of the required additional system programs (and additionally\par
   several that were removed to preserve storage space on the RX01.\par
   The RX01 media is a significantly smaller storage device than a\par
   DECtape).\par
\par
e) The \i MARK12\i0  and \i TC12F\i0  programs were added to the RX01 system\par
   programs directory for use when this system is booted to a\par
   PDP-12.\par
\par
f) The various directory structures were set to appropriate block\par
   limits as required on the RX01 media.\par
\par
Note: All aspects of creating P?S/8 for the RX01 were performed on the PEPS SIMH package for Windows.\line\f0\page\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f1\par
Glossary of Terms\par
\par
 1.\tab BIN format\par
\par
\tab The standard binary loading format in the DEC \ul\i Paper-tape\par
\ulnone      \ul operating system\ulnone\i0  is known as \i BIN\i0  format which is optimized for\par
\tab loading data from paper-tape frames.  The format includes both\par
\tab leader and trailer frame codes to delineate sets of data; the\par
\tab highest tape channel is used as a visual aid to help position\par
\tab paper-tapes in the reader; all leader and trailer frames have \tab only the high-order bit punched.  Data frames may or may not have\par
\tab this bit punched, but clearly have some combination of the other \tab bits also punched.  All leader frames are ignored. \tab Loading\par
\tab ends when the first trailer frame is encountered.  \i BIN\i0  format\par
 \tab incorporates all necessary elements needed to load 12-bit\par
\tab words into memory including a 12-bit checksum at the end of the\par
\tab tape; memory field change frames are included as required.\par
\par
\tab Note: field change frames have the two highest bits punched. \tab Additionally, field change frames are not included in the overall\par
 \tab tape checksum.  It is surmised this came about because the format\par
\tab was upgraded when machines larger than 4K memory were first\par
\tab produced by then, this could have compatibility issues with \i BIN\i0  \tab format tapes produced somewhat earlier.\line\line\tab BIN format is inherently compromised in terms of loading\par
\tab efficiency because of the need to support leader and checksum\par
\tab codes that are superfluous in an operating system environment\par
\tab such as P?S/8.  Storage devices inherently have device parity\par
\tab checking for all data; leader and trailer frame codes serve no\par
\tab purpose when data is stored in blocks on a mass \tab storage device.\par
\par
\tab While certain systems use an adaptation of BIN format for binary\par
\tab files, P?S/8 uses the more versatile and efficient \i Slurp \i0 binary\par
\i\tab\i0 loading format described elsewhere; \i Slurp\i0  format is optimized for\par
 \tab efficient loading.  The P?S/8 binary utility program (also known\par
\tab as \i BIN\i0 ) includes utilities to convert between paper-tape data and\par
\tab binary file data (in both conversion directions).\par
\par
 2.\tab Burness\par
\par
\tab Jack Burness is one of the original members of the \ul\i WFCMPG\ulnone\i0\par
\tab (described elsewhere in this document).  He created most of the\par
\tab\i DIAL\i0  operating systems for the PDP-12 (described elsewhere in\par
\tab this document).  Additionally, he produced the 8K BASIC\par
\tab modifications of 4K BASIC (and LAB-8/E BASIC, which is actually\par
\tab the same source code file with a global variable changed to \tab create either version).\par
\par
\tab Most notably, Mr. Burness is one of the first four people to\par
\tab reject working on the defective demo program that eventually\par
\tab became released as OS/8.  (This is described in more detail\par
\tab elsewhere in this document.)\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\par
\par
 3.\tab DEC\par
\par
\tab\i DEC\i0  is the usual abbreviation of Digital Equipment Corporation,\par
\tab the company that created the PDP-8 and other computers.\par
\par
\tab Note: If you are not already familiar with this, you probably\par
\tab have little reason to read this document.\par
\par
 4.\tab DECtape\par
\par
\tab\i DECtape\i0  is a removable media storage device based on the earlier\par
\tab\i LINCtape\i0  used on the classic \i LINC\i0 , \i LINC-8\i0  and \i PDP-12\i0  as an\par
\tab embedded subsystem of these machines.\par
\cf3\par
\tab\cf0 Note: While the media used for both \i DECtape\i0  and \i LINCtape\i0  is\par
\tab nominally the same magnetic media, the formats differ greatly.\par
\tab The most obvious difference is that using \i DECtapes\i0 , the supply\par
\tab reel is mounted on the left side of the drive (while \i LINCtapes\i0\par
\tab spin in the opposite direction).  Where applicable, plastic\par
\tab canisters for tape storage exist;  by convention, blue plastic\par
\tab parts are used for \i DECtapes\i0  while green plastic parts are used\par
\tab for \i LINCtapes\i0 .  (This is unreliable as the convention is often\par
\tab violated.)\par
\par
\tab\i DECtapes\i0 , much like \i LINCtapes\i0 , are completely block-replaceable \tab and can be considered to be analogous to more typical rotating\par
\tab disk drives; however, block placement is for more critical due to\par
\tab the serial nature of having to locate a new block by traversing\par
\tab all intervening blocks starting from the present position.\par
 \tab Additionally, there are considerations regarding the choice of\par
\tab initial search direction; using the wrong initial direction will\par
\tab cause delays due to the need to turn the tape around, etc.\par
\par
\tab More successful software operating systems use performance-\par
\tab enhancing techniques; less successful operating systems can take \tab profoundly longer to accomplish roughly analogous tasks.\line\line\tab A related issue is the ability to transfer multiple blocks in the\par
\tab same handler call.  Once a block is not accessed during the\par
\tab latest read or write operation, the entire operation must be\par
\tab restarted; assuming the next block is adjacent to the last block\par
 \tab transferred, the delay will be palpable.  As such, more\par
\tab successful software combines multiple block transfers in the same\par
\tab transfer operation.\par
\par
\tab\i LINCtapes\i0  can only transfer data moving in the forward direction. \tab Both formats can search while moving in reverse; however, \i DECtape\i0\par
\tab can also transfer data while moving in the reverse direction.  \tab Certain software utilities take advantage of reverse data\par
\tab transfers, especially on other DEC machines based on wider\par
\tab word lengths.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Unlike \i LINCtapes\i0 , the count of words in a \i DECtape\i0  block is\par
\tab subject to a restriction of being a whole multiple of 18-bit\par
\tab words.  As such, the standard format for the PDP-8 is \tab 129 12-bit\par
\tab words (or 86 18-bit words).\par
\par
\tab The underlying format of \i DECtape\i0  first used on the PDP-1 and\par
\tab PDP-4 was changed with the release of the type 552 controller\par
\tab originally designed for the \i PDP-5\i0  (a precursor design that\par
\tab predates the PDP-8).  As such, these two systems are merely\par
\tab compatible with each other and cannot share data with other\par
\tab machines that support \i DECtape\i0 .\par
\par
\tab Note: For information on a tentative solution to this problem,\par
\tab see the description of the \i LINC-8\i0  elsewhere in this document.\par
\cf3\par
\cf0\tab With the exception of very early \i Typeset-8\i0  systems, all PDP-8\par
\tab models equipped with \i DECtape\i0  use a compatible \i DECtape\i0  controller\par
\tab and one or more compatible \i DECtape\i0  drives.\par
\par
\tab Note: For more information on the type 552 \i DECtape\i0  controller and\par
\tab the type 555 dual \i DECtape\i0  drive, see the description of the\par
\tab\i Typeset-8\i0  operating system elsewhere in this document.\par
\par
\tab Note: For more information on \i DECtape\i0  controllers, see the\par
\tab appropriate entry (elsewhere in this document) for one of the\par
\tab following:\line\line\tab a) TC01\par
\par
\tab b) TC08\par
\par
\tab c) TD8E\par
\par
\tab For more information on \i DECtape\i0  drives, see the appropriate entry\par
\tab (elsewhere in this document) for one of the following:\par
\par
\tab a) TU55\par
\par
\tab b) TU56\par
\par
\tab The P?S/8 operating system \i standard-bearer\i0  configuration is\par
\tab designed for use with \i DECtape\i0  as implemented on the TC01 (or\par
\tab TC08) \i DECtape\i0  controller and TU55 (or TU56) drives.\par
\line\tab Note: For more information on the rudiments of the P?S/8\par
\tab operating system, see the P?S/8 entry elsewhere in this document.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
 5.\tab DIAL\par
\par
\tab\ul\i LAP6-DIAL\ulnone\i0  and \ul\i DIAL-MS\ulnone\i0  are related operating systems for the\par
\tab PDP-12 only.  These systems are partially (and primarily\par
\tab cosmetically) based on former systems for the classic LINC and\par
\tab LINC-8 that are 100% constructed of LINC code; \i DIAL\i0  takes\par
\tab advantage of the PDP-8 processor and largely consists of PDP-8\par
\tab code using LINC mode programming (only) to access the peripherals\par
\tab (which cannot be directly accessed using PDP-8 coding).\line\line\tab\i LAP6-DIAL\i0  only runs from LINCtapes, whereas \i DIAL-MS\i0  can be\par
\tab configured to include a choice of several hard disk drives that\par
\tab may be available on the same machine.  Either system is always\par
\tab started from a standard bootstrap of two words entered on the\par
\tab front panel switches; if relevant, this will perform a reboot to \tab the hard disk (if present).\line\line\tab Both systems can only support a maximum of 8K of memory (despite\par
\tab the fact that the PDP-12 supports up to 32K memory).\par
\line\tab\i LAP6-DIAL\i0  can run in 4K of memory while \i DIAL-MS\i0  requires 8K.  The\par
\tab largest program that can be assembled (and saved) is the entirety\par
\tab of fields 0 and 1 without restriction.\par
\par
\tab Note: The reason there is no apparent memory restriction is that\par
\tab all programs are required to start in one of a small number of\par
\tab designated memory locations.\par
\par
\tab There are actually minor restrictions on the contents of memory\par
\tab which must be preloaded with a small series of startup\par
\tab instructions associated with the designated startup locations,\par
 \tab etc.\par
\par
\tab Due to the potential presence of support routines for the\par
\tab supported hard disk options, programs that load into the highest\tab\tab addresses of field 1 may require loading from a system configured\par
\tab for LINCtape exclusively; since the system is always started by\par
\tab booting a LINCtape, the hard disk routines can be reloaded\par
\tab afterwards.\par
\par
\tab Note: These conventions are in sharp contrast with PDP-8\par
\tab standards which generally reserve (at least) locations 07600-\par
\tab 07777 as (the primary section of) the system kernel.\par
\par
\tab\i DIAL\i0  files can be converted from/to P?S/8 using the P?S/8\par
\tab\i L6DCON\{vert\}\i0  utility.  \i L6DCON\i0  can only be run from P?S/8 systems\par
\tab running on the LINC-8 or PDP-12.  (The program includes its own\par
\tab internal LINCtape handlers for both systems.)  More information\par
\tab about \i L6DCON\i0  can be obtained from other documents such as the\par
\tab\ul\i P?S/8 System Programs Guide\ulnone\i0  available separately.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\line\line\tab Note: With the exception of P?S/8 and OS/8, virtually all\par
\tab operating systems based on LINCtapes use 256 12-bit words/block\par
\tab LINCtapes.  Various total block counts are associated with either\par
\tab standard or custom usage for any of these systems, etc.\par
\par
\tab Most of both \i DIAL\i0  systems was created by Jack Burness, who, as a\par
\tab student at Brooklyn Poly was a member of the \ul\i WCFMPG\ulnone\i0 .  (See\par
\tab elsewhere in this document for more information regarding the\par
\tab\ul\i WCFMPG\ulnone\i0 .)\par
\par
 6.\tab Disk Monitor System\par
\par
\tab The \ul\i Disk Monitor System\ulnone\i0  is a rather poor operating system for\par
\tab certain arbitrary configurations of the PDP-8.  Certain of the\par
\tab internal design principles make excessive use of minimally\par
\tab available resources.  Most notably, performance is only tolerable\par
\tab on the fastest disks available at the time:\line\line\tab a) RF08\line\line\tab b) DF32\par
\par
\tab This system also runs on TC01/TC08 DECtape using TU55 or TU56 \tab drives.  The DECtape handling is simply horrid.  Because of the\par
\tab internal design limitations, it is impossible to transfer\par
\tab multiple blocks in a single handler call.  As such, each DECtape\par
\tab read/write block transfer must be individually searched.\par
\tab Additionally, it is highly likely that blocks are actually\par
\tab (nearly) adjacent (and certainly no attempt is made to create a\par
\tab proper inter-block offset).  This invariably means that the next\par
\tab call to the same handler will require a reverse search to before\par
\tab the last block transferred, followed by a turn-around to transfer\par
\tab the desired block in the forward direction.  This behavior is\par
\tab sometimes referred to as either \i shoe-shining\i0  or \i yo-yo\i0  motion.\par
\line\tab While this behavior describes a small subset of the block search\par
\tab behavior of OS/8 and P?S/8 (and the\ul\i  DECtape Library System\ulnone\i0  that\par
\tab predates all three of the stated systems), these systems support\par
\tab attempts to transfer multiple blocks in one call without the\par
\tab turn-around overhead associated with \b\i every\b0\i0  tape operation in the\par
\tab Disk Monitor environment.\par
\par
\tab Note: At some point in time, an advanced user of both the \ul\i Paper-\ulnone\i0\par
\tab\ul\i tape operating system\ulnone\i0  software and the \ul\i Disk Monitor System\ulnone\i0\par
\tab pointed out that a skilled operator can develop software on the\par
\tab high-speed reader/punch \b\i faster\b0\i0  than on the \ul\i Disk Monitor System\ulnone\i0 .\line\line\tab Some of the poor design aspects of the \ul\i Disk Monitor System\ulnone\i0  were\par
\tab demanded by ignorant managers causing the design of what\par
\tab ultimately was released as OS/8 to be forever ruined in certain \tab ways.  Ultimately, it can be shown that all of these notions are\par
\tab superfluous.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: Despite this, OS/8 (which also requires 8K memory) is a\par
\tab somewhat better performing system than the \ul\i Disk Monitor System\ulnone\i0 .  \tab The forthcoming P?S/8 \i SHELL\i0  overlay will define a system\par
\tab largely analogous to OS/8 with these defective design notions\par
\tab removed.  The present basic P?S/8, as implemented and released as\par
\tab Version 8Z, already illustrates some of these issues.\par
\par
\tab Here is a single case in point:\line\line\tab The \ul\i Disk Monitor System\ulnone\i0  restarts by saving a portion of memory\par
\tab\b\i every\b0\i0  time the system restarts at 07600; however, only rarely is\par
\tab this feature constructively required.\par
\par
\tab This concept is mimicked in OS/8 because of the original\par
\tab managerial edict, etc.\line\line\tab In P?S/8 8Z, restart at 07600 is a read-only operation; the\par
\tab system device can be write-locked (for safety) if desired.  When\par
\tab the user desired the contents of memory to be saved, memory\par
\tab should be loaded with the \i GET\i0  command.  All of Field 0 will be\par
\tab saved in the system scratch area reserved for this purpose.\par
\tab Following this operation, the usual read-only restart is\par
\tab performed.  System commands exist to manipulate the saved core\par
\tab image in various ways, including saving designated memory\par
\tab locations to binary output files, and performing debugging\par
\tab sessions using P?S/8 ODT (which implements a proper superset of\par
\tab OS/8 ODT).  If desired, conforming programs can be run as\par
\tab impromptu versions of P?S/8 system programs by invoking the\par
\tab appropriate command.\par
\par
\tab In the P?S/8 \i SHELL\i0  this concept will be extended in a compatible\par
\tab way to allow any useful operation; however, the basic concept\par
\tab will be supported at every level of implementation.\par
\par
\tab As such, this fundamental design point as implemented in the Disk\par
\tab Monitor System is superfluous and actually somewhat dangerous to \tab use, yet cannot be prevented.\line\line\tab The \ul\i Disk Monitor System\ulnone\i0  is based on a highly inflexible design\par
\tab restriction:  The candidate device must be capable of being\par
\tab expressed in logical blocks that are 129 words long.  This is\par
 \tab\b\i not\b0\i0  because the system is based on storage allocation tables.\par
\tab Instead, the 129th word is required as an simple-minded\par
\tab implementation of the link to the next block in a sequence of\par
\tab blocks that encompass a file.\line\line\tab Unfortunately, this mechanism only works for the DF32 and RF08\par
\tab head-per-track hard disks because they can be organized on a word\par
\tab basis.  PDP-8 DECtape requires this format (which is clearly the\par
\tab inspiration for the closed-end design).\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab All more modern PDP-8 storage devices are based on storage blocks\par
\tab that are exact multiples of powers of two quantities of 12-bit \tab words.  As such, the \ul\i Disk Monitor System\ulnone\i0  cannot be implemented on\par
\tab other devices supported by P?S/8, OS/8 and other systems.\par
\par
 7.\tab DTORG\par
\par
\tab The \i DTORG\i0  directive is a versatile feature of the PAL language\par
\tab (at least in theory).  It was first used by the Typeset-8 group\par
\tab for the purpose of specifying a DECtape (or other storage device)\par
\tab block intended to be used as part of a special-purpose loader of\par
\tab (up to 128 12-bit) words intended to be written to a designated\par
\tab block on a target system device.\par
\par
\tab At the time, the symbolic name \i DTORG\i0  was appropriate as the\par
\tab Typeset-8 bootable application system only ran on PDP-8 format\par
\tab DECtapes on first the type 552 DECtape controller and then later\par
\tab the TC01 or TC08 DECtape controller.  For more information, see\par
\tab the appropriate description of the TC01 or TC08 DECtape\par
\tab controllers elsewhere in this document.  The symbol suggests an\par
\tab\ul\i OR\ulnone i\ul G\ulnone in\i0  (meaning a designated storage block) on \ul\i D\ulnone EC\ul T\ulnone ape\i0 . \tab However, the concept can be applied to any application that is \tab oriented towards logical blocks that are 128 12-bit words long.\par
\par
\tab Note: \i DTORG\i0  is not normally used in OS/8 because the logical\par
\tab records are 256 words long; as such, this would require some\par
\tab additional features to create an aligned double-page memory space\par
\tab in the corresponding loading program.  Virtually all other\par
\tab operating systems (with the exception of the \ul\i Disk Monitor System\ulnone\i0  \tab which uses 129 words per block) designed for the PDP-8 can \tab theoretically make use of this feature.\line\line\tab\i DTORG\i0  was first implemented on the \ul\i TOPS-10 PAL10\ulnone\i0  cross-assembler.\par
 \tab Unfortunately, the Typeset-8 group made unreasonable demands on \tab the design of \i PAL10\i0  such that the binary output, which attempts\par
\tab to punch binary paper-tape output in \i BIN\i0  format, creates\par
 \tab defective output when the DTORG directive is used.\line\line\tab This largely went unnoticed because it has no direct effect on\par
\tab any other program that runs on \i TOPS-10\i0 ; however, this makes the\par
\tab DTORG directive virtually useless for any other application.\par
\par
\tab Some time later, managerial edicts were made regarding \ul\i OS/8 PAL8\ulnone\i0 , \par
\tab which would seem to be the OS/8 equivalent of \i PAL10\i0 .\par
\par
\tab Note: This is actually not true.  There are numerous\par
\tab incompatibilities between \i PAL8\i0  and \i PAL10\i0 .  For the most part,\par
\tab P?S/8 \i PAL\i0  is fully compatible with \i PAL10\i0 ; however, when\par
\tab programming for \i PAL8\i0  compatibility, certain language elements\par
\tab must be avoided.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\par
\tab\cf0 While the goal was to provide an alternate development\par
\tab environment for the \i Typeset-8\i0  programmers, the implementation of\par
\tab\i DTORG\i0  in \i PAL8\i0  was done in a heavy-handed manner; as such, binary\par
\tab output of PAL8 cannot be loaded by any OS/8 utility if DTORG is\par
\tab used in the source code.\line\line\tab Note: For the purpose of advancing the development of P?S/8 in\par
\tab general, there are proposed changes to this situation that can\par
\tab satisfy all interested groups (with some cooperation).  For more\par
\tab information, consult the \ul\i P?S/8 PAL Version 8S Technical Guide\ulnone\i0  \tab available as a separate document.\par
\par
 8.\tab DUMP\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 \i DUMP\i0  is a small versatile utility program that is included\par
\tab in the P?S/8 starter system.\line\line\tab\i DUMP\i0  is used for a small number of specific tasks initiated by \tab commands as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) DU\{MP\} and display the contents of a designated block on the\par
\tab    system device or any other logical unit controlled by the\par
\tab    system device.  The display consists of a formatted display of\par
\tab    (up to) the entire contents of the designated block where each\par
\tab    line includes the relative index number of the left-most value\par
\tab    followed by the eight values starting from that particular\par
\tab    relative index; to the right of the octal display is a\par
\tab    character display representing the same values in six-bit\par
\tab    ASCII text.  (The character display format was chosen\par
\tab    arbitrarily because it is the most likely text display to be\par
\tab    relevant to the contents of various P?S/8 system blocks.)  The\par
\tab    output is always directed to the system console device (which\par
\tab    may be redirected to the Logical Console Overlay if enabled).\par
\par
\tab    The display can be limited to the lowest portion of the\par
\tab    potential display as indicated above by the setting of the\par
\tab    computer front panel switches.  If desired, the /S command-\par
\tab    line option switch can be used to override this; the value of\par
\tab    \i =xxxx\i0  passed on the command line will be used for all\par
\tab    displayed output.  If the \i =xxxx\i0  is not explicitly passed, the\par
\tab    value 0000 will be used (which will display all output).\par
\par
\tab b) TR\{ANSFER\} a set of contiguous blocks from any accessible\par
\tab    logical device unit and starting block to another accessible\par
\tab    logical device unit and starting number.\line\line\tab    Note: Only advanced users should INVOKE this option as\par
\tab    transfers that overlay certain system blocks can cause\par
\tab    permanent damage to the operating system.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab    The transfer mode checks for available memory when DUMP is\par
\tab    first loaded into memory.  In 4K, the largest number of blocks\par
\tab    that can be transferred in any one operation is a function of\par
\tab    the program length.  As of P?S/8 Version 8Z, the largest\par
\tab    read/write transfer allowed is 23 blocks per command.\cf3\par
\par
\tab    \cf0 DUMP fully conforms to P?S/8 extended memory allocation rules.\par
 \tab    On certain systems, the first 3/4 of Field 1 may also be\par
\tab    available; in this case, DUMP can always transfer 24 blocks\par
\tab    because the read/write transfer occurs in 10000-15777.\par
\par
\tab    For all systems that run on 8K (or larger) memory, the largest\par
\tab    transfer is 32 blocks (an entire memory field) because this is\par
\tab    the largest value that can be supported on any supported\par
\tab    configuration (generally as a hardware restriction).\par
\par
\tab c) ZA\{P\} (or patch) the contents of a designated block on any\par
\tab    accessible logical device unit.  The designated block is first\par
\tab    displayed in the same format as described above for the DU\{MP\}\par
\tab    command.\par
\par
\tab    Various single-character commands can be used to examine and\par
\tab    change the contents of any of the relative locations in the\par
\tab    range of 000-177.  As such, it is possible to completely\par
\tab    change the contents of the block being patched.\par
\par
\tab    Note: As stated above with regard to the TR\{ANSFER\} operation,\par
\tab    only advanced users should invoke this option as patching\par
\tab    certain system blocks can cause permanent damage to the\par
\tab    operating system.\par
\par
\tab d) EX\{IT\} is used to terminate the \i DUMP\i0  utility and reload P?S/8.\par
\par
\tab Note: All P?S/8 system configurations handle logical units in\par
\tab particular ways.  The general unit range is 0 through 7; however,\par
\tab depending on system device handler specifics, certain logical\par
\tab units may cause permanent errors (which will require some form of\par
\tab restart) or certain logical units may be anomalous to other\par
\tab logical units.\par
\par
\tab In general, these anomalies arise due to certain bits of the\par
\tab three-bit field expressing the unit (throughout any particular\par
\tab configuration of P?S/8) being ignored.  For example, the RX01\par
\tab variation of P?S/8 will support units 0 through 3 on some\par
\tab hardware configurations and units 0 through 1 on others.  For all\par
\tab supported RX01-compatible configurations, the high-order bit of\par
\tab the unit is always ignored.  As such, units 4 through 7 are\par
\tab anomalous to units 0 through 3.  Future releases of the RX01-\tab based system may include better internal testing of the unit bits\par
\tab to cause all unavailable logical unit bits to permanently cause\par
\tab errors.  For the present implementation, it is up to the user \tab to understand which logical units are available, etc.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\par
\cf0\tab Most importantly, it is possible to fully automate all \i DUMP\i0  \tab operations through the use of .DUMP command input files passed to\par
\tab the program.\par
\par
\tab .DUMP files are ASCII text files compatible with the TFS file\par
\tab structure of P?S/8.\par
\par
\tab Note: The designation of .DUMP files refers to the way they are\par
\tab stored in other file structures; this is used to indicate the\par
\tab contents are valid commands to the P?S/8 DUMP utility.  The\par
\tab actual files (once stored on a P?S/8 system) are ordinary text\par
\tab files that can be prepared (or modified) with the P?S/8 embedded\par
\tab text editor.\par
\par
\tab .DUMP files can (and \b should!\b0 ) include internal commentary; any\par
\tab line within a .DUMP file starting with the \cf2\highlight3\f0\\\cf0\highlight0\f1  character is\par
\tab considered a comment and will otherwise be ignored.\line\line\tab The operations of the P?S/8 starter system make extensive use of\par
\tab DUMP controlled by .DUMP files to create initial structures, etc.\par
\tab This is done to facilitate adding on additional system programs\par
\tab and other settings to produce a complete release.\par
\par
\tab Note: Future releases of P?S/8 will use alternate techniques to\par
\tab create system releases.  For more information, see the\par
\tab description of the DTORG directive elsewhere in this document.\par
\par
 9.\tab DW8E\par
\par
\tab The \i DW8E\i0  is a (partially compatible) bus convertor that can be\par
\tab attached to PDP-8 models that predate the Omnibus machines.  The\par
\tab DW8E-P is for positive bus machines such as the PDP-12 and the\par
\tab PDP-8/L and positive bus PDP-8/I systems (that are exceedingly\par
\tab rare).\line\line\tab The DW8E-N is for the PDP-8, LINC-8 and the PDP-8/I (with the\par
\tab more common negative bus).\par
\par
\tab Note: The LINC-8 cannot provide DMA support for the DW8E-N\par
\tab because of the LINC CPU; only the program transfer portion is\par
\tab supported.\par
\par
\tab The only DMA peripheral supported is a modified version of the\par
\tab RK8E known as the RK8F, which is compatible with the RK8E except\par
\tab for the specific DMA interface modifications for the DW8E\par
\tab specifically.\line\line\tab Additionally, certain Omnibus peripherals support instructions of \tab the form \i 6xy0\i0  which are not supported on the DW8E.  For certain \tab peripherals, this may be a problem.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\par
\par
10.\tab Elekman\par
\par
