\tab Note: The type 552 DECtape controller was designed for the PDP-5; however that computer model was an extremely poor seller (and a net loss to DEC).  Worse still, the design was deficient and did not allow extended memory support; as an after-thought, the extended memory control for the DECtape block data transfers was connected to the Data Field bits.  This is not a viable solution since if interrupts are on at the time of the transfer, any actual interrupt clears the Data Field.  As such, interrupts must be disallowed, etc.\line\line\tab This was of no consequence to the Typeset-8 group as all of their application machines (sold to customers) were only 4K; as such, this was a way to get rid of dead inventory as it was very unlikely the customers would ever realize the restriction was a sign of second-rate hardware.\par
\par
\tab Additionally, the type 555 DECtape drive pair was also in the same state of only a few systems could support the device; they were designed to work together, although the Typeset-8 software really had no utilities requiring a second drive.\line\line\tab Later systems replaced the dual 555 drive with a single TU55 drive which occupies the same rack space.  By proper internal hardware configuration, the TU55 could be retrofitted for use with the type 552 controller; when the TC01 DECtape controller was available, newer systems used the newer controller with the same TU55 drive reconfigured for the newer controller.\par
\par
\tab Note: DEC generally kept their Typeset-8 customers in the dark regarding the specific hardware; each customer system was a custom release.\par
